---
/**
 * Subscription confirmed.
 *
 * A P1 page from the specification's page list, and the destination a double opt-in provider
 * redirects to once someone clicks the confirmation link. It exists because that reader has
 * just taken an action with no visible result: without a page here they land on the homepage
 * and cannot tell whether it worked.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY UNLINKED. It is the one page on the site with no inbound link anywhere, and
 * that is correct rather than an oversight: nothing links to it because nothing should.
 * `SITE.newsletterAction` is still empty, so no provider is redirecting here yet; when one
 * is connected, this URL goes into its confirmation-redirect setting. It is noindex, so it
 * cannot be reached from search either. The orphan sweep reports it, and that is the entry
 * to ignore.
 *
 * The most useful thing it does is the second step. A new subscription that lands in a spam
 * folder is indistinguishable from no subscription, so this page's job is to send the reader
 * to the whitelisting instructions while the email is still at the top of their inbox - which
 * is the moment they will actually do it.
 *
 * No address is read, stored, logged or reflected: the page has no query handling at all, so
 * there is nothing to leak if a provider does pass one.
 */
import Sidebar from "../components/Sidebar.astro";
import { SITE } from "../consts";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { numbersFor, published } from "../lib/post";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import { fitTitle, fitDescription } from "../lib/meta";

const posts = published(await getCollection("blog"));
const numbers = numbersFor(posts);
const latest = posts.slice(0, 3);

const title = "You are on the list";
const description =
  "Your subscription to Landed Math is confirmed. Here is what arrives, how often, and the one step that keeps it out of your spam folder.";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebPage",
  name: `${title} — ${SITE.name}`,
  description,
  url: `${SITE.url}/confirmed/`,
  robots: "noindex",
};

/** The cadence, stated the way the newsletter form states it, so the two agree. */
const WHAT_ARRIVES = [
  "One email when a tracked change actually moves a per-unit cost — not a daily digest.",
  "The figure, its unit, its period, the named primary source, and the date it was retrieved.",
  "The arithmetic, so you can change an input and see the answer move.",
  "An unsubscribe link in every message, and no address ever shared or sold.",
];
---

<BaseLayout title={`${fitTitle(title, "", SITE.name)} — ${SITE.name}`} description={fitDescription(description)} jsonLd={jsonLd} noindex>
  <div class="container">
    <main id="main">
      <div class="page-head">
        <h1>You are on the list</h1>
        <p>
          The subscription is confirmed. Nothing else is required — but there is one step worth
          doing now, while the email is still at the top of your inbox.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout">
        <span class="callout-label">Do this now, it takes a minute</span>
        <p>
          Mark the confirmation email as <strong>not spam</strong> and add the sender to your
          contacts. A subscription that lands in a spam folder is indistinguishable from no
          subscription, and the first message from any new publication looks to a filter like
          the shape of spam.{" "}
          <a href="/whitelist/"><strong>Client-by-client instructions →</strong></a>
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="section-title">What arrives, and how often</h2>
      <ul>
        {WHAT_ARRIVES.map((w) => (
          <li>{w}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p>
        The cadence is irregular by design. An email goes out when a tracked change moves a
        cost, which is not most days — a daily digest would have to invent something to say on
        the days when nothing did.
      </p>

      <h2 class="section-title">While you wait</h2>
      <p>
        The {posts.length} analyses published so far are all free to read in full, and the{" "}
        <a href="/brief/">archive</a> lists them by the day they went out. If you have an
        invoice in front of you, the <a href="/calculator/">calculator</a> does the per-unit
        arithmetic without an email address.
      </p>
      <ul>
        {latest.map((p) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`/blog/${p.id}/`}>{p.data.title}</a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <h2 class="section-title">If the email never arrives</h2>
      <p>
        Check the spam folder first, then{" "}
        <a href="/contact/">say so</a> — the site sends no tracking pixels, so it cannot detect
        that a message went astray. That is a deliberate trade:{" "}
        <a href="/privacy/">the privacy page</a> promises no tracking, which means delivery
        problems have to be reported rather than noticed.
      </p>
    </main>

    <Sidebar numbers={numbers} />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  ul {
    padding-left: 20px;
  }
  li {
    margin: 7px 0;
  }
</style>
